/E (This lab shows how to create a dynamic web application by pairing PHP, MySQL, and a standard web browser. PHP executes on the server rather than inside the visitor's browser, while MySQL organizes structured data into relational tables of rows and columns. Working together, PHP queries and processes records from MySQL and delivers the resulting web pages to the browser.)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
1006 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 383 0 R
/E (For this project, I created a compact school management portal called Beacon Academy, backed by a MySQL database named school_db administered via phpMyAdmin in a local environment. Authentication supports two roles: teachers have full read and write access to attendance, grades, and student profiles, whereas students can browse largely the same views on a read-only basis without making edits.)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
